BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260825T220000Z
DTEND:20260825T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260825T000000Z
SUMMARY:3D Printer Orientation Part 2 (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:Learn how to 3D print from idea to design to fabrication! In 
 this two part orientation series\, you will learn how to design and 3D 
 print your own ideas.\n\nNote: You must sign up and attend both orientation 
 parts to gain 3D printer access. If you have take one (but not both) of the 
 orientations\, you can register for this set and only attend the 
 orientation part you are missing.\n\nThe in-person orientation is a great 
 choice for those who need extra support with navigating a computer. A 
 laptop will be provided.\n\n \n\n\nIf you are under the age of 13 you must 
 have a parent or guardian present to sign up for 3D Printer OS to send 
 prints to the library.\n\n \n\n \n\n3D Printing Orientation Part 1: 3D 
 Design - Tuesday\, August 18th\, 6-7:30pm\n\n\n	Learn about the basics of 
 3D printing and how to create your own design.\n\n\n3D Printing Orientation 
 Part 2: Printing Designs - Tuesday August 25th\, 6-7:30pm\n\n\n	Learn about 
 our specific 3D printers and the software you use to send a print.\n\n\n 
 \n\nRegistration required.
LOCATION:Computer Lab\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Kyle Lampart":MAILTO:klampart@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:3D Printing, Innovation Lab, Maker, STEAM
UID:LibCal-14288-17140838
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17140838
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260825T220000Z
DTEND:20260825T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260825T000000Z
SUMMARY:CROWS!
DESCRIPTION:CROWS! \n\nWho? What? Where? When? Why? \n\n \n\n\n\nThe only 
 bird that “murders”... \n\nAble to recognize and remember faces for 
 decades... \n\nThe same brain-weight-to-body ratio as humans...and probably 
 as self-aware as humans... \n\nHolding funerals for their dead... \n\n 
 \n\n\n\nWho is as smart as a seven-year-old human child? What is the major 
 cause of death of crows? Where do crows play? When do crows use tools? Why 
 are crow's bioindicators or sentinel species for West Nile Virus?  \n\n 
 \n\n\n\nJoin Kyra Thurow Bartow\, Animal Husbandry & Intern Manager at 
 Piedmont Wildlife Center\, to learn more about these canny corvids!  
 \n\nCheck out the booklist Clever Crows! \n\n\n\nKyra Thurow Bartow is an 
 avid birder and environmental educator who loves sharing all about the 
 wonders of the bird world. She works as the Animal Husbandry & Intern 
 Manager at Piedmont Wildlife Center in Durham\, NC\, where she takes care 
 of their ambassador animals\, including raptors\, a raven\, reptiles\, and 
 small mammals. In her free time\, she can be found birding\, crafting\, 
 reading\, and spending time with her husband\, Alan\, and their cat\, 
 Canary\, and dog\, Smokey. \n\n \n\nTuesday August 25th starting at 6 p.m. 
 in the Durham County Main Library auditorium located at 300 North Roxboro 
 Street in Durham.       \n\nParking is available in the library parking lot 
 or at the North Queen Street Garage located at 115 North Queen Street which 
 is behind the apartments directly across the street from the library. 
 Parking at the garage is free. Please bring your parking ticket to a 
 service point for validation.         \n\nRegistration is appreciated to 
 receive an email reminder\, cancellation link\, and program updates. 
 Seating is first come first served. Registration does not hold or guarantee 
 a seat.       \n\nThere will be no live animals or insects at this event.   
   \n\n  \n\nQuestions? Email mkoslofsky@dconc.gov \n\n \n\nNote: If you 
 have any accessibility questions or accommodation needs\, please contact me 
 at mkoslofsky@dconc.gov or call 919-560-9311. Please make all requests 
 three business days prior to the event to ensure I can provide the best 
 accommodations possible!\n\n \n\nImage description: Glossy black American 
 crow sitting on a worn fence post looking into the distance. \n\nPhoto 
 credit: Warren Bielenberg
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Megan Koslofsky":MAILTO:mkoslofsky@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:
UID:LibCal-14288-15433339
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/15433339
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260825T223000Z
DTEND:20260825T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260825T000000Z
SUMMARY:Big Kid Storytime
DESCRIPTION:Geared toward kids and tweens\, ages 7-12! Join us every 
 Tuesday evening for the next chapter in our selected chapter book reading! 
 A snack will be served.\n\nListening to books has serious benefits for 
 developing readers. It helps tweens improve their pronunciation and 
 fluency\, improves focus and memory\, increases attention span\, builds 
 comprehension skills\, and can boost mental health by relieving stress and 
 calming thoughts. \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n
LOCATION:Southwest Regional - Children's Programming Room\, Southwest Regional Library (3605 Shannon Rd)
ORGANIZER;CN="Caroline P.":MAILTO:cbpeterson@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Story Time
UID:LibCal-14288-16599458
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16599458
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260825T223000Z
DTEND:20260825T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260825T000000Z
SUMMARY:Shelf Sessions w/ Leffty
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an evening in the library as we listen to some live 
 and local music. \n\nIt is sort of like Tiny Desk\, but just in the library 
 :)\n\nOut of North Carolina\, Leffty is an American band founded by drummer 
 Joshua Davell Scott and vocalist Kingsley Priester. Blending modern 
 alternative\, R&B\, and soul\, the band creates a sound that feels 
 atmospheric\, intimate\, and emotionally raw. Drawing inspiration from 
 artists like Coldplay\, The 1975\, and Frank Ocean\, Leffty lives in the 
 space between vulnerability\, groove\, and introspection.\n\nThe name 
 Leffty comes from the idea of embracing what makes you different. Like 
 being left-handed - uncommon and sometimes misunderstood - the band 
 believes that uniqueness isn't something to hide\, but something to lean 
 into.\n\nThrough each song\, Leffty explores emotions that are often 
 suppressed and stories that often go unspoken. Their music creates space 
 for listeners to reconnect with themselves\, sit with honesty\, and 
 ultimately feel again.\n\n
LOCATION:Main Children's Programming Room\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Alexandra Wurst":MAILTO:awurst@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Business, Community Engagement, Durham, Music
UID:LibCal-14288-17111439
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17111439
END:VEVENT
