BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260908T140000Z
DTEND:20260908T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260908T000000Z
SUMMARY:Tuesday Computer Class @ Main
DESCRIPTION:Come join a supportive group environment for self-paced digital 
 skills courses. We'll be using Northstar Digital Literacy\, a free online 
 resource available to all Durham County Library patrons. A library staff 
 member will be present to get you set up and answer questions as each 
 participant moves through online modules on their own at their own pace. 
 Northstar allows you to track your learning progress and earn badges or 
 certificates for completing a course. These classes are best for learners 
 who have at least basic keyboard and mouse skills. Our computer lab has a 
 limited number of laptops for use\, so bringing your own laptop and 
 headphones is encouraged.\n\nComputer and digital skills topics 
 include:\n\n\n	Phone Keyboard Basics & Logging In\n	Basic Computer 
 Skills\n	Internet Basics\n	Using Email\n	Windows 10 & 11\n	Mac 
 OS\n	Microsoft Word\, Excel\, and PowerPoint\n	Google Docs\n	Social 
 Media\n	Information Literacy\n	Career Search Skills\n	Your Digital 
 Footprint\n	Accessing Telehealth Appointments\n	Cybersecurity 
 Basics\n\n\nThese classes are self-paced and each week participants will 
 choose which lessons they want to complete on their own. You do not need to 
 have come to the first class to attend the following weeks and there is no 
 requirement to commit to all of the classes through October. Register for 
 the weeks that work best for you!\n\nIf you have any questions or 
 accommodation needs\, contact Jimmy McKinnell at jmckinnell@dconc.gov or 
 call 919-560-9940.\n\n\nÚnase a un ambiente de apoyo para cursos de 
 habilidades digitales a su propio ritmo. Usaremos Northstar Digital 
 Literacy\, un recurso en línea gratuito que también está disponible en 
 español para todos los usuarios de la Biblioteca del Condado de Durham. Un 
 miembro del personal de la biblioteca estará presente para ayudarle a 
 comenzar y responder sus preguntas mientras cada participante avanza por 
 los módulos en línea de manera individual y a su propio ritmo. Northstar 
 le permite llevar un registro de su progreso y obtener insignias o 
 certificados al completar un curso. Estas clases son ideales para personas 
 que ya cuentan con habilidades básicas de teclado y mouse. Nuestro 
 laboratorio de computación tiene una cantidad limitada de laptops 
 disponibles\, por lo que se recomienda traer su propio laptop y 
 audífonos.\n\nLos temas de computación y habilidades digitales 
 incluyen:\n\n\n	Conceptos básicos del teclado del teléfono e inicio de 
 sesión\n	Habilidades básicas de computación\n	Conceptos básicos de 
 Internet\n	Uso del correo electrónico\n	Windows 10 & 11\n	Mac 
 OS\n	Microsoft Word\, Excel y PowerPoint\n	Google Docs\n	Redes 
 sociales\n	Alfabetización informacional\n	Habilidades para la búsqueda de 
 empleo\n	Su huella digital\n	Cómo acceder a citas de telesalud\n	Conceptos 
 básicos de ciberseguridad\n\n\nEstas clases son autodirigidas y cada 
 semana los participantes elegirán qué lecciones desean completar por su 
 cuenta. No necesita haber asistido a la primera clase para participar en 
 las siguientes sesiones\, y no es necesario comprometerse a todas las 
 clases hasta octubre. ¡Inscríbase en las semanas que mejor le 
 funcionen!\n\nSi tiene preguntas o necesita alguna adaptación\, 
 comuníquese con Jimmy McKinnell a jmckinnell@dconc.gov o llame al 
 919-560-9940.
LOCATION:Computer Lab\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="James McKinnell":MAILTO:jmckinnell@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Computer Class, Digital/Tech Literacy
UID:LibCal-14288-17421719
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17421719
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260908T140000Z
DTEND:20260908T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260908T000000Z
SUMMARY:Declaration of Independence: Rare Prints on Display
DESCRIPTION:\n\nWe invite you to view rare 19th-century prints of the 
 Declaration of Independence at Durham County Main Library.\n\nAs part of We 
 the People: A Duke & Durham Initiative\, Durham County Main Library and the 
 Nasher Museum of Art at Duke University are presenting three historic 
 copies of the Declaration of Independence on loan from the David M. 
 Rubenstein Americana Collection. This exhibition offers a rare opportunity 
 to explore how the nation's founding document was visually rendered and 
 circulated in early American history.\n\n\n\nThe exhibition will feature 
 three rare prints on loan from the David M. Rubenstein Americana 
 Collection: a rare official full-sized 1823 facsimile engraved by William 
 J. Stone (known as a Stone Declaration) alongside two historic broadside 
 copies from the same era: an 1818 reproduction by calligrapher Benjamin 
 Owen Tyler and a more ornate 1819 illustrated version published by John 
 Binns. \n\nVisitors can also engage with interactive activities and explore 
 featured library resources on display alongside the historic 
 prints.\n\n\n\n \n\nGallery Hours at Durham County Main 
 Library\n\nSeptember 8\, 2026 – September 20\, 2026\n\nMonday – 
 Wednesday: 10:00 AM – 7:00 PM\n\nThursday – Saturday: 10:00 AM – 5:00 
 PM\n\nSunday: 1:30 PM – 5:00 PM\n\n*Gallery entry is on the 2nd floor of 
 Main Library*\n\n\n\n \n\nGroup Visits & Planning Your 
 Entry\n\nPre-registered group visits (10  people) take place Monday – 
 Friday from 10:00 AM – 3:30 PM. Groups of 10  may sign up for self-guided 
 times here.\n\nIndividual visitors are welcome at all times\, but please 
 note you may experience a brief wait to enter if the gallery is at capacity 
 or if a large group is inside.\n\n\n\n \n\nRead With Us\n\nUS 
 Constitution/Declaration of Independence Childrens' Booklist \n\nUS 
 Constitution/Declaration of Independence Adult Booklist \n\n \n\nExhibition 
 Related Programming\n\nRedeeming the Promise of the Declaration of 
 Independence: Louis Austin\, the Black Press\, and the 20th Century Freedom 
 Struggle - Wednesday\, September 9\, 2026 at 6 p.m.\n\nHer Fight\, His 
 Name: The Story of Gwen Carr and Eric Garner - Thursday\, September 10\, 
 2026 at 6:30 p.m.\n\nThe American Revolution at 250: Historians Discuss the 
 Many Worlds of 1776 - Tuesday\, September 15\, 2026 at 6 p.m.\n\n 
 \n\n\n\nFull Durham Schedule\n\nTo maximize access across the region\, the 
 documents are hosted in two Durham spaces:\n\n\n	Durham County Main 
 Library: September 8 – September 20\, 2026\n	Nasher Museum of Art at Duke 
 University: September 24\, 2026 – January 3\, 2027 - click here for more 
 information\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Declaration of Independence is organized in 
 collaboration with Duke Arts and the Nasher Museum of Art and is part of We 
 the People: A Duke & Durham Initiative that marks the 250th anniversary of 
 the signing of the Declaration of Independence through a series of public 
 events examining the nation’s evolving identity. Three historic copies of 
 the Declaration of Independence are on loan from the David M. Rubenstein 
 Americana Collection. Leadership support for We the People has been 
 generously provided by David M. Rubenstein. \n\n\nInformation on Entering 
 Main Library\n\nParking is available in the library parking lot or at the 
 North Queen Street Garage located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind 
 the apartments directly across the street from the library.\n
LOCATION:Main Library - Auditorium - Upper Corridor 2nd Floor\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Celeste Holtzmann":MAILTO:choltzmann@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:
UID:LibCal-14288-17394096
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17394096
END:VEVENT
