BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260909T140000Z
DTEND:20260909T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260909T000000Z
SUMMARY:Declaration of Independence: Rare Prints on Display
DESCRIPTION:\n\nWe invite you to view rare 19th-century prints of the 
 Declaration of Independence at Durham County Main Library.\n\nAs part of We 
 the People: A Duke & Durham Initiative\, Durham County Main Library and the 
 Nasher Museum of Art at Duke University are presenting three historic 
 copies of the Declaration of Independence on loan from the David M. 
 Rubenstein Americana Collection. This exhibition offers a rare opportunity 
 to explore how the nation's founding document was visually rendered and 
 circulated in early American history.\n\n\n\nThe exhibition will feature 
 three rare prints on loan from the David M. Rubenstein Americana 
 Collection: a rare official full-sized 1823 facsimile engraved by William 
 J. Stone (known as a Stone Declaration) alongside two historic broadside 
 copies from the same era: an 1818 reproduction by calligrapher Benjamin 
 Owen Tyler and a more ornate 1819 illustrated version published by John 
 Binns. \n\nVisitors can also engage with interactive activities and explore 
 featured library resources on display alongside the historic 
 prints.\n\n\n\n \n\nGallery Hours at Durham County Main 
 Library\n\nSeptember 8\, 2026 – September 20\, 2026\n\nMonday – 
 Wednesday: 10:00 AM – 7:00 PM\n\nThursday – Saturday: 10:00 AM – 5:00 
 PM\n\nSunday: 1:30 PM – 5:00 PM\n\n*Gallery entry is on the 2nd floor of 
 Main Library*\n\n\n\n \n\nGroup Visits & Planning Your 
 Entry\n\nPre-registered group visits (10  people) take place Monday – 
 Friday from 10:00 AM – 3:30 PM. Groups of 10  may sign up for self-guided 
 times here.\n\nIndividual visitors are welcome at all times\, but please 
 note you may experience a brief wait to enter if the gallery is at capacity 
 or if a large group is inside.\n\n\n\n \n\nRead With Us\n\nUS 
 Constitution/Declaration of Independence Childrens' Booklist \n\nUS 
 Constitution/Declaration of Independence Adult Booklist \n\n \n\nExhibition 
 Related Programming\n\nRedeeming the Promise of the Declaration of 
 Independence: Louis Austin\, the Black Press\, and the 20th Century Freedom 
 Struggle - Wednesday\, September 9\, 2026 at 6 p.m.\n\nHer Fight\, His 
 Name: The Story of Gwen Carr and Eric Garner - Thursday\, September 10\, 
 2026 at 6:30 p.m.\n\nThe American Revolution at 250: Historians Discuss the 
 Many Worlds of 1776 - Tuesday\, September 15\, 2026 at 6 p.m.\n\n 
 \n\n\n\nFull Durham Schedule\n\nTo maximize access across the region\, the 
 documents are hosted in two Durham spaces:\n\n\n	Durham County Main 
 Library: September 8 – September 20\, 2026\n	Nasher Museum of Art at Duke 
 University: September 24\, 2026 – January 3\, 2027 - click here for more 
 information\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Declaration of Independence is organized in 
 collaboration with Duke Arts and the Nasher Museum of Art and is part of We 
 the People: A Duke & Durham Initiative that marks the 250th anniversary of 
 the signing of the Declaration of Independence through a series of public 
 events examining the nation’s evolving identity. Three historic copies of 
 the Declaration of Independence are on loan from the David M. Rubenstein 
 Americana Collection. Leadership support for We the People has been 
 generously provided by David M. Rubenstein. \n\n\nInformation on Entering 
 Main Library\n\nParking is available in the library parking lot or at the 
 North Queen Street Garage located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind 
 the apartments directly across the street from the library.\n
LOCATION:Main Library - Auditorium - Upper Corridor 2nd Floor\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Celeste Holtzmann":MAILTO:choltzmann@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:
UID:LibCal-14288-17394097
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17394097
END:VEVENT
