SUMMARY:Her Fight\, His Name: The Story of Gwen Carr and Eric Garner
DESCRIPTION:\n\n\n\nIn conjunction with The Declaration of Independence 
 Display we invite you to join us for a special event!\n\nCommunity 
 Screening: Her Fight\, His Name: The Story of Gwen Carr and Eric 
 Garner\n\n\n\n\n\nBrought to you by: Archive of Documentary Arts and David 
 M. Rubenstein Rare Book & Manuscript Library\n\nThe short documentary 
 centers on Gwen Carr's enduring fight for justice following the 2014 death 
 of her son\, Eric Garner\, after being pinned to the ground in a chokehold 
 by a police officer in Staten Island. The New York City medical examiner 
 stated the cause of death as a homicide. Garner's final words\, "I can't 
 breathe\," became a rallying cry in protests against police violence and 
 systemic racism across the United States. Filmed over several years\, the 
 documentary places Carr's voice at its core\, tracing her transformation 
 from grieving mother to nationally recognized advocate for accountability 
 and human rights.\n\nWinner of the 2026 Human Rights Digital Storytelling 
 Award sponsored by the David M. Rubenstein Rare book and Manuscript 
 Library.\n\n\n\nCommunity discussion to follow with Director Brad Bailey 
 and Mrs. Gwen Carr.\n\n\nRead With Us\n\nUS Constitution/Declaration of 
 Independence Childrens' Booklist \n\nUS Constitution/Declaration of 
 Independence Adult Booklist \n\n\n\nFull Durham Schedule\n\nTo maximize 
 access across the region\, the documents are hosted in two Durham 
 spaces:\n\n\n	Durham County Main Library: September 8 – September 20\, 
 2026\n	Nasher Museum of Art at Duke University: September 24\, 2026 – 
 January 3\, 2027 - click here for more information\n\n\n\n\n\nThe 
 Declaration of Independence is organized in collaboration with Duke Arts 
 and the Nasher Museum of Art and is part of We the People: A Duke & Durham 
 Initiative that marks the 250th anniversary of the signing of the 
 Declaration of Independence through a series of public events examining the 
 nation’s evolving identity. Three historic copies of the Declaration of 
 Independence are on loan from the David M. Rubenstein Americana Collection. 
 Leadership support for We the People has been generously provided by David 
 M. Rubenstein. \n\n\nInformation on Entering Main Library\n\nParking is 
 available in the library parking lot or at the North Queen Street Garage 
 located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind the apartments directly 
 across the street from the library.
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
